  Title                    = {Multiple {B}ernoulli Relevance Models for Image and Video Annotation},
  Author                   = {S. Feng and R. Manmatha and V. Lavrenko},
  Booktitle                = cvpr,
  Year                     = {2004},

  Abstract                 = {1002--1009},
  File                     = {:feng04cvpr.pdf:PDF},
  Owner                    = {verbeek},
  Review                   = {Use Bernoulli instead of multinomial to avoid reducing probs of annot keywords when there are many relevant terms.
Multinomial can reflect importance of words in document, not the case for keyword annotation, maybe it is for text.


Image divided in rectangular regions, claim that it is better than relying on segmentation methods.

generative mixture model
- uniform prior over training images = mixture components
- Gaussian mixture over visual region features: centered at training region descriptors, all same istropic cov. matrix.
- Bernoulli over annotation bits: Bayes estimate with corpus wide prior: (mu*delta + Nv) / (mu + N) : Nv nr of images with bit on, N number of images, delta presence in this particular image, mu is set using held out data to weight the prior.

Evaluation per word, and then averaged:
o On Corel, use 5 words per image (with top Bernoulli score for that image), compute precision and recall over images for that word.
o MAP: rank images with Bernoulli prop of the assigned keywords (top 5 per image).},
  Timestamp                = {2009.02.09}
}

