@InProceedings{fidler13cvpr,
  Title                    = {Bottom-up Segmentation for Top-down Detection},
  Author                   = {S. Fidler and R. Mottaghi and A. Yuille and R. Urtasun},
  Booktitle                = cvpr,
  Year                     = {2013},

  File                     = {fidler13cvpr.pdf:fidler13cvpr.pdf:PDF},
  Review                   = {Uses a variety of SIFT descriptors and extensions that are pooled over regions by means of (elementwise) max of avrg over the outerproducts of the features.

Once features are computed, they learn an SVM regression model to predict the overlap with ground truth segments, using not only the VOC'11 segmentations, but additional complete segmentations on all training images from Berkeley [32].

The last step consists in compositing the segments from different classes into a single segmentation image. 
This is done by overlaying progressively lower scoring segments, where the top scoring segments occlude lower scoring ones.
A threshold is set to retain on average the right amount of objects per image, which is used to clip the initial list of segments.
Once the first segment is selected, later ones are included only if they surpass a progressively more stringent threshold that increases by a constant (0.02) after each segment is added.

The expected output for VOC segmentation (2012 version) is an index image with values 0--20 for bg and the 20 classes.
Performance for VOC segmentation is measured as follows per class using an intersection over union measure
correct pixels for the class / (pixels labeled either in the GT or the prediction as that class).}
}

