@InProceedings{jia11nips,
  Title                    = {Heavy-tailed distances for gradient based image descriptors},
  Author                   = {Y. Jia and T. Darrell},
  Booktitle                = nips,
  Year                     = {2011},

  Owner                    = {verbeek},
  Review                   = {Places compound gamma-laplace model over difference between matching sift-descriptors or patch-prototype difference.
Leads to a heavier tail of the distriibution than using the Laplace model directly, this heavier tail better fits the observed data.

Also refers to using the square root "to alleviate the effect of heavy tails"

Proposes to consider the likelihood ratio of data x and y to (1) be generated by the same parameter p(x|m_xy)p(y|m_xy) and (2) to be generated by separate prameters p(x|m_x)p(y|m_y). The three parameters are inferred by maximum likelihood.
The distance is then taken as sqrt(-log( LHR ))

They estimate the hyper parameters by treating a set of observations as iid samples from the compound model: they do not share tha latent laplace scale across any observations.

Experiments on sift matching of photo-tourism data set shows significantly better matching performance, L1 is worse, and L2 is again worse than both.},
  Timestamp                = {2012.06.16}
}

