@InProceedings{jurie11iccv,
  Title                    = {Visual Word Disambiguation by Semantic Contexts},
  Author                   = {F. Jurie},
  Booktitle                = iccv,
  Year                     = {2011},

  File                     = {jurie11iccv.pdf:jurie11iccv.pdf:PDF},
  Review                   = {Uses context information in 2 ways

1) Global: classify image (regions) to belong or not to certain classes (objects, scenes, materials)
Size: nr of context classes

2) Local: associate class probs with local features based on the region classifications associated with regions that cover them
For each visual word, consider the context class which maximizes the t-test statistic between weighted counts obtained on positive and negative images.
Represent vis word by weighted count of most discriminative context class.
Thus the association of visual words to context classes depends on the classification task at hand.
Representing visual words by weighted count of more than a single context class helps little.
Size: nr of visual words.

Main improvement due to local contextual coding, adding in global context feature helps moderately ontop of that.}
}

