@Article{koenderink99ijcv,
  Title                    = {The Structure of Locally Orderless Images},
  Author                   = {J. Koenderink and A. Van Doorn},
  Journal                  = ijcv,
  Year                     = {1999},
  Number                   = {2/3},
  Pages                    = {159-168},
  Volume                   = {31},

  File                     = {koenderink99ijcv.pdf:koenderink99ijcv.pdf:PDF},
  Review                   = {read 25/4/2012

the locally orderless representation underlies both our low-level and high-level representations (eg SIFT and BoW)

Despite that locally the data is modeled orderless, it is in general far from iid due to the spatial regularity of images.

Therefore, non-iid models seem useful in this context, even more so at small local scales (smaller than complete image / patch).

We can derive a locally orderless representation at different spatial granularities, and model different spatial neighborhoods as iid, meaning
that we assume samples in a local bag to be non-iid, but the various local-bags to be iid from each other.
The FV gradient representation would be the sum of local Polya gradients, computed over the local regions

Alternatively we can build a hierarchical model.
At level 1 we map local features to BoW histograms computed over a limited spatial support
The level1 histograms can be quantized by a mixture of Dirichlet model, into another histogram representation.
We can continue doing this, each level has a growing spatial support, and quantizes the histograms from the previous level.
In this case we could use a FV gradient representation over each level, where parameters are shared across the local regions in a level.

Another possibility to construct a hierarchical represenatation is to quantize the FV representation of one level into a next histogram representation.}
}

